WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:10.500
Should unemployment insurance, or UI for
short, cover paid family leave? President

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:12.990
Trump's budget includes a proposal to do
just that.

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:17.070
New parents would get UI checks for
up to six weeks after the birth or

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:21.689
adoption of a child. That sounds good, but
it also would require higher taxes on

00:00:21.689 --> 00:00:28.349
jobs, and add administrative complexity
to an already strained program. In 2018, a

00:00:28.349 --> 00:00:32.420
third of UI recipients exhausted
benefits without finding a new job, and

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:37.170
almost one in eight benefit dollars were
paid in error. That's hundreds of

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:41.070
thousands of unemployed people
exhausting benefits, and over three

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:45.630
billion dollars in misspending. The
administrators running the unemployment

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:49.950
insurance program need to focus on
reconnecting UI recipients to work, and

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:54.270
making sure payments are going to those
who really need them. There are lots of

00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:58.440
ideas for how to help families with paid
leave, but adding a paid leave program

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:02.370
for employed people to the unemployment
insurance program designed to help

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:05.520
unemployed people doesn't make much
sense.

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:07.980
Do you think the unemployment

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:10.400
insurance programs should cover paid
family leave?

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:12.000
Let us know in our poll.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:16.400
Also, let us know what other topics you'd
like our scholars to cover in 60 seconds,

00:01:16.409 --> 00:01:22.850
and be sure to like and subscribe for
more research and videos from AEI.

